		},
		{
			"id":"855f009f4a9a83d4",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"**Stimulus:**\n=> blood glucose concentration rises above normal\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-610,
			"y":180,
			"width":304,
			"height":140,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"dbcb1238d7a0bd87",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"**Receptor(also control center)**\nIslets of Langerhans in the pancreas detect it + are stimulated\n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-233,
			"y":-160,
			"width":387,
			"height":163,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"429e6d05d9f1e0bc",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"**Corrective Mechanism**\n![[Pasted image 20240903131703.png|300]]\n* Islets of Langerhans => secrete more insulin into the bloodstream. \n* Blood transports insulin to liver and muscles\n\t* Insulin increases permeability of cell membrane to glucose. \n\t\t* Glucose is absorbed more quickly by cells\n\t* Insulin increases rate of respiration\n\t* Insulin causes liver and muscles to convert excess glucose to glycogen (for storage). Glycogen is stored in liver and muscles. ",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":360,
			"y":50,
			"width":680,
			"height":630,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"dc9cddd925068dfe",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Blood Glucose concentration decreases BACK TO NORMAL. this provides a **feedback** to the receptor (Islets of Langerhans) to reduce insulin production. ",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-233,
			"y":680,
			"width":349,
			"height":176,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"fc9c8f1e2e2eca7d",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"**What happens when Blood glucose concentration rises above normal level**\n=> glucose absorbed into bloodstream in SI. \n",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-860,
			"y":-280,
			"width":500,
			"height":120,
			"color":"2"
		},
		{
			"id":"2f65aa3285487abe",
			"type":"text",
