		},
		{
			"id":"8dc0104f54366df7",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"**Stimulus**\nBlood glucose concentration falls below normal",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-673,
			"y":1980,
			"width":313,
			"height":109,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"1937db10978b352e",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"**Receptor(control center)**\n=> islets of Langerhans in the pancreas are stimulated",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-260,
			"y":2260,
			"width":300,
			"height":140,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"1bd0aecbc3257fc8",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"**Corrective Mechanism**\n![[Pasted image 20240903132317.png|300]]\n* Islets of Langerhans secrete more glucagon into the bloodstream. \n* Blood transports Glucagon to liver and muscles\n\t* Glucagon causes conversion of stored glycogen back into glucose\n\t* From liver, glucose enters bloodstream",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":180,
			"y":1626,
			"width":540,
			"height":494,
			"color":"5"
		},
		{
			"id":"d291c24ccb709fa9",
			"type":"text",
			"text":"Blood glucose concentration increases BACK TO NORMAL. this provides a feedback to the receptor (Islets of Langerhans) to reduce glucagon production",
			"styleAttributes":{},
			"x":-167,
			"y":1340,
			"width":415,
			"height":180,
			"color":"5"
		},
